def intro(R):
    return [
        ('h1', '1. Introduction'),
        ('p', "High-entropy alloys (HEAs), or more generally multi-principal element alloys (MPEAs), abandon the "
              "single-base-element paradigm of conventional metallurgy and combine several elements in comparable "
              "proportions [@yeh2004,cantor2004]. The resulting composition space is vast, and it contains alloys with "
              "exceptional combinations of strength, ductility, fracture resistance and high-temperature capability "
              "[@miracle2017,george2019,senkov2010]. Because only a minute fraction of this space can be synthesized and "
              "tested, data-driven surrogate models that map composition and processing to properties have become a central "
              "tool for alloy design [@liu2023,zhang2026]."),
        ('p', "Machine-learning (ML) models have been trained to classify phase formation [@huang2019,zhou2019,pei2020,kaufmann2020] "
              "and to regress hardness [@wen2019,chang2019,yang2022,chen2025,sheikh2025,zhou2026] and yield strength "
              "[@giles2022] of MPEAs, and several studies have used them to propose and synthesize new alloys "
              "[@wen2019,yang2022,rao2022,zhou2026]. Reported accuracies are often impressive—coefficients of determination "
              "above 0.9 are common for hardness—but they are almost always obtained by random *k*-fold cross-validation or "
              "a random hold-out split. Random splitting answers the question \"how well does the model interpolate among "
              "alloys like those it was trained on?\" Alloy design asks a different question: \"how well will the model "
              "predict an alloy, or an alloy system, that nobody has made yet?\""),
        ('p', "The two questions have different answers when the data are clustered, and MPEA data are strongly clustered. "
              "Experimental campaigns explore an alloy system systematically (e.g. Al_{x}CoCrFeNi with varying *x*), the same "
              "nominal composition is re-reported after different processing or by different groups, and yield strength is "
              "often measured at several temperatures on one alloy. A random split therefore places near-copies of most test "
              "alloys in the training set, a form of information leakage known to inflate performance estimates across "
              "ML-based science [@kapoor2023,roberts2017]. In materials informatics, leave-cluster-out and forward "
              "cross-validation [@meredig2018,xiong2020] and systematic out-of-distribution benchmarks on computed databases "
              "[@li2023,li2025,omee2024,dunn2020] have shown that random-split metrics can grossly overstate discovery "
              "performance, and best-practice guides recommend grouped validation [@wang2020]. A recent review of ML for HEAs "
              "makes the same recommendation [@zhang2026]. However, to our knowledge no study has quantified how large the "
              "optimism of random validation actually is for experimental MPEA mechanical properties, whether the ranking "
              "of popular model families changes when extrapolation is required, or how the answer depends on the kind of "
              "novelty (a new composition, a new system, a new element, a new year of literature or a new laboratory)."),
        ('p', "Equally important is whether a model knows when it is wrong. Uncertainty estimates allow a materials scientist "
              "to decide which predictions to act on and which candidates require confirmation, and they drive active "
              "learning [@tran2020,palmer2022]. Gaussian processes and ensembles provide model-based uncertainty, and "
              "conformal prediction provides intervals with finite-sample coverage guarantees for any model "
              "[@vovk2005,lei2018,barber2021,angelopoulos2023]. The guarantee, however, assumes that calibration and test "
              "data are exchangeable—precisely the assumption that discovery violates. Materials applications of conformal "
              "prediction have so far been evaluated on random splits [@boruah2026], so it is unknown whether the "
              "advertised coverage survives extrapolation to new alloy systems. Distance-based applicability-domain (AD) "
              "measures [@sutton2020,janet2019] offer a complementary, model-agnostic warning signal whose value for MPEAs "
              "has not been established."),
        ('p', f"Here we address these questions with a benchmark built entirely on open data. We curated "
              f"{R['HV_n']} room-temperature hardness and {R['YS_n']} yield-strength records from the open MPEA database "
              f"[@borg2020] and assembled an independent, out-of-domain test set of {R['GE_n']} refractory alloys from a "
              f"different laboratory [@detor2022]. Nine regressors, from *k*-nearest neighbours to the tabular foundation "
              f"model TabPFN [@hollmann2025], were evaluated under six protocols of increasing extrapolation severity with "
              f"nested hyper-parameter selection whose inner folds reproduce each deployment scenario, and with system-level "
              f"resampling for all statistical inference. The specific contributions are: (i) a quantification of "
              f"how much random cross-validation overstates the accuracy of MPEA hardness and yield-strength models for new "
              f"compositions, systems, elements, publication years and laboratories; (ii) evidence on which model families "
              f"and feature representations remain useful under extrapolation; (iii) a demonstration that conformal intervals "
              f"calibrated with random folds lose their coverage under extrapolation, and an evaluation of how far "
              f"calibration folds that reproduce the deployment scenario recover it—empirically, since no coverage guarantee "
              f"holds under such shifts; and (iv) an applicability-domain and screening analysis "
              f"that translates these findings into practical rules for model-guided alloy selection. All data, splits, "
              f"predictions and code are released to make the benchmark a reusable reference for future MPEA models."),
    ]
